"label": "The showdown and Biff's epiphany",
"where": "Act Two, pp. 101-106",
"tag": "present",
"setting": "The yard, then the Loman kitchen, late Tuesday night.",
"summary": "Biff takes the hoe from Willy and says goodbye. He says he is leaving for good and will not write. Willy refuses to shake his hand. He tells Biff that he is ruining his life out of spite, which means to hurt someone on purpose. Biff then puts the rubber hose on the table. Willy pretends not to know what it is. Biff says he will not feel sorry for him.\n\nBiff tells everyone the truth. He says that he was in jail in Kansas City for stealing a suit. He says that he has stolen himself out of every good job because Willy filled him with hot air and made him think he should be a boss in two weeks. Biff then tells what happened after he ran from Oliver's office. As he ran down the stairs of that office building, he suddenly stopped. He saw the sky and thought about the things he loves: work, food, time to sit and smoke. He asks why he is grabbing for something he does not want.\n\nBiff cries out that he is a dime a dozen, which means ordinary, and so is Willy. Willy shouts back, \"I am not a dime a dozen! I am Willy Loman!\" Biff says that Willy was never anything but a hard-working drummer. Then Biff's anger runs out, and he breaks down sobbing, holding on to Willy. Biff begs Willy to burn the phony dream before something happens, and then he goes upstairs. Willy is astonished and says that Biff likes him. Linda says that he loves him.",
"why": "This is Biff's moment of self-knowledge and the true center of the play.",
"keyMoments": [
"Biff places the rubber hose on the table and refuses to pity Willy (p. 104).",
"Biff admits he was in jail and blames Willy's hot air for his failures (pp. 104-105).",
"Biff describes seeing the sky and what he truly loves (p. 105).",
"Biff says he and Willy are each a dime a dozen (p. 105).",
"Biff cries in Willy's arms, and Willy is amazed that Biff loves him (p. 106)."
]
