"examQuestions": [
{ "q": "Which statement best describes the play's structure?", "options": ["It is told in strict chronological order over several years", "It is a single present-tense scene in one room", "It moves between Willy's present day and his memories, often without a break", "It is told by a narrator who is not part of the family"], "answer": 2, "why": "The play flows between a twenty-four-hour present and Willy's memories, helped by lighting, music, and the transparent set." },
{ "q": "Which pairing correctly matches a symbol with its meaning?", "options": ["Stockings: Willy's guilt over the affair", "Flute: Howard's modern office", "Seeds: Ben's diamonds", "Refrigerator: Biff's high school success"], "answer": 0, "why": "The stockings tie Willy's guilt to the Boston affair and Linda's mending. The other pairings are mismatched." },
{ "q": "Which argument best supports reading Willy as a tragic hero?", "options": ["He fully understands his flaws before he dies", "He is a king whose fall changes a nation", "He fights fiercely to keep his dignity and sense of worth, and he pays with his life", "He is cheated by Howard and has no responsibility"], "answer": 2, "why": "Miller argued that a common man can be tragic when he struggles to claim his rightful dignity. Option A is false, since Willy lacks insight." },
{ "q": "What is the main effect of the wall-lines in the set?", "options": ["They help the audience tell present scenes from memories", "They make the house look expensive", "They hide the actors", "They show that the house is haunted"], "answer": 0, "why": "In present scenes actors use the door; in memories they walk through walls (stage directions, p. 1)." },
{ "q": "Biff says, 'He had the wrong dreams.' Which reading is best?", "options": ["Willy should have dreamed of money instead", "Willy's goals were based on appearance and success, not on who he truly was", "Willy never wanted anything", "Biff blames Charley for Willy's dreams"], "answer": 1, "why": "Biff means that Willy chased social ideas of success and never learned who he was (p. 111). Biff is not blaming Willy as a villain." },
{ "q": "Which is an example of dramatic irony?", "options": ["Willy thinks Biff's tears mean love will be repaid by his insurance money, while the audience sees it will cost him his life", "Linda mends her stockings", "Happy takes a girl to dinner", "Charley asks for cards"], "answer": 0, "why": "The audience sees the cost of Willy's plan while Willy feels sure that he has found a perfect solution." },
{ "q": "How does Charley represent a different version of the American Dream?", "options": ["He believes in quick riches", "He gets rich through luck and charm", "He succeeds through steady work and does not need to be adored", "He rejects work and money"], "answer": 2, "why": "Charley runs a business, raises a successful son, and helps Willy, all without needing to be well liked. Bigsby calls this a dream of hard work and charity." },
{ "q": "Why is the Boston scene central to the play?", "options": ["It explains how Willy earned his money", "It shows the moment Biff's faith in his father collapsed and explains why Willy feels guilty", "It introduces Ben", "It shows Willy's firing"], "answer": 1, "why": "The affair explains Biff's change after high school and Willy's guilt, which keeps interrupting the present." },
{ "q": "What is the function of the Requiem?", "options": ["To show Willy's imaginary funeral", "To introduce a new character", "To give the family and neighbors a final chance to argue about Willy's life and its meaning", "To take place thirty years later"], "answer": 2, "why": "The Requiem lets Linda, Biff, Happy, and Charley judge Willy and show how each will go on." },
{ "q": "Which term best describes a style that shows inner states on stage through a transparent set and changing lights?", "options": ["Naturalism", "Satire", "Pastoral", "Expressionism"], "answer": 3, "why": "Expressionism makes the stage a picture of the character's mind. Naturalism tries to copy everyday life exactly." },
{ "q": "According to Bigsby, why is the debate over whether the play is a tragedy or a social play partly beside the point?", "options": ["The play is neither", "Both can be true, as in the Oedipus plays or Hamlet: a flawed man in a flawed society", "Miller refused to answer", "Only critics care about it"], "answer": 1, "why": "Bigsby argues that Willy can be both a flawed individual and a victim of a flawed society." },
{ "q": "Which of the following is a reasonable argument that Willy is NOT a tragic hero?", "options": ["He is brave and struggles", "He dies in the end", "He never gains self-knowledge, and Biff is the one who does", "He is a family man"], "answer": 2, "why": "Bigsby notes that Miller himself wished that Willy had more insight, and that it is Biff who reaches understanding." }
],
