"modelParagraph": "Miller uses the staging of memory to show that Willy's guilt controls his present. The opening stage directions explain that in present scenes the actors respect the walls of the house, but in scenes of the past they step through them. This simple rule tells the audience when Willy's mind has slipped. The motif of The Woman's laughter works in the same way. We first hear it in Act One while Linda is talking, and Willy suddenly moves toward a different part of the stage. We do not yet know who she is, but we feel his guilt. When the Boston scene finally appears in Act Two, the laugh and the stockings take on their full meaning. Bigsby adds that these memories are not accurate flashbacks but constructions that Willy edits to suit his needs. The form therefore matches the theme: for Willy, the past never stays in the past.",
"annotation": "This paragraph connects a technical detail (the wall-lines) to a motif and to the theme of guilt. It uses terms accurately and gives Bigsby's idea in a paraphrase, not a long quote.",
