"modelParagraph": "When Linda says that attention must finally be paid to such a person, she is defending Willy against his own son. She has just heard Biff call Willy crazy, and she answers by saying that Willy is not a great man but a human being to whom a terrible thing is happening. The line asks her sons to see his exhaustion instead of his flaws, and then she proves her point with facts: Willy has lost his salary, he borrows from Charley, and he has tried to kill himself. By insisting on attention, she also speaks for Miller's idea that an ordinary person's suffering is serious enough for tragedy. Bigsby argues that Linda is not a weak doormat but a fighter, and this speech supports his view. The rest of the play tests whether Biff, Happy, Howard, or anyone else will give Willy that attention.",
